The Linux operating system acts as an interface between computer hardware and application software by managing and abstracting hardware resources. The uname command is used to obtain information about the operating system and Linux kernel, such as the kernel version and system architecture. The lscpu command provides information about the CPU, including the processor architecture, number of CPUs, cores, and threads. Linux uses this CPU information through its process scheduler to allocate CPU time among different running processes. The lsblk command displays storage devices, partitions, their sizes, and mount points, showing how Linux manages physical storage through block devices and filesystems. The ps command displays information about currently running processes, including their Process IDs (PIDs), CPU usage, memory usage, and process status, demonstrating the role of Linux in process management. The top command provides real-time information about CPU utilization, memory usage, system load, and running processes, allowing users to monitor how system resources are being used. Linux also abstracts memory by providing virtual memory to processes so that applications do not need to directly manage physical RAM. Similarly, storage devices are accessed through files and directories instead of applications directly accessing physical disk sectors. Input and output devices such as keyboards, USB devices, displays, and network devices are managed by the Linux kernel through device drivers. Therefore, Linux hides the complexity of the underlying hardware and provides standard services to applications through the kernel. The commands uname, lscpu, lsblk, ps, and top demonstrate how the operating system identifies hardware, manages CPU and memory resources, controls storage, and monitors processes. Overall, the Linux operating system acts as a resource manager and abstraction layer between applications and hardware, allowing multiple programs to use system resources efficiently, safely, and conveniently.
